 the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c60668bb-7444-51a7-9d21-131099a908b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305022 #edb/ref 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )={x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(2\\beta +2\\gamma )={x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =-1,\\gamma =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305057 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305057 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305057 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305057 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}-x-1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305057 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41a72880-8cb5-5bdc-96f8-c709119673ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305057 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305785 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305785 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305785 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4099c08f-db10-5b94-b476-722ec92e2f91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305805 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305805 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305805 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "424f3742-f4f4-58bf-9771-5a09636ce05b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306503 #edb/ref 1080 "y={e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306503 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306503 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8ea666d9-fb1e-528b-9c1a-2a72695635a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306732 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306732 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306732 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a7e7bafe-af2c-5d92-a1f9-afdc8f293f04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306856 #edb/ref 1080 "y=-2{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x+1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306856 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306856 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b6a9087d-0776-582b-a88f-17e01518a957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 